We get sets of experiments for different combinations of number of threads that
launch compute tasks and number of patches handled by launch. In the simple
one-thread case, we make thread 0 of a multithreading environment submit 
different kernels realisations for a certain number of patches. Some of these
kernels are parallelised internally, others are not. Once these tests are 
complete, we allow two threads to issue the same type of kernel in parallel.
This way, we can stress test the system.

Per realisation, we get nine measurements:

1. The first one is the total time of a single kernel execution.
2. The second entry is the update per degree of freedom within a single kernel.
3. The third entry measures the total runtime of the parallel launching. This
   time includes the setup and freeing of any temporal data structures. It also
   measures until all threads have finished their work. Therefore, this value
   should be always be bigger than the first one. However,
4. the fourth entry gives us the time per degree of freedom update if we launch
   multiple kernels in parallel.
5. the fifth entry gives the time spent solely within the compute kernels.
6. the sixth entry gives the compute kernel time per degree of freedom.
7. The seventh, eighth and nineth entry provide the raw data, so we can see the standard
   deviation of the measurements. We also see that we usually sample each value
   multiple times to eliminate noise.
   
If a single kernel invocation (for multiple patches) from one thread is able to
exploit all cores already, then multiple launches of a kernel should either 
deliver the same throughput or a worse one, as there's some overhead. If a 
single kernel launch (for multiple patches) is not able to exploit all threads,
launching multiple kernels in parallel should improve the throughput.


# Visualisation


The directory hosts a script called visualisation.py which solely takes the 
output piped into a file as input. It delivers plots similar to the two 
below:

@image html example-vis.png
@image html example-vis-log.png

These scatter plots report the throughput for particular combinations of 
threads which launch kernels simultaenously and patches per kernel launch.
We distinguish the time per kernel (y-axis) and the total time for all
kernels (x-axis) and label one best-case configurations combining these
two metrics (so something that's good in both metrics). 

Plots like the one above highlight that some kernel realisations deliver 
outcomes for their patches particularly quickly. They exhibit a small
algorithmic latency. Other realisations are not that fast for their 
patches, but are fairer and hence allow Peano to pipe more kernels 
simultatenously through the system.



# Example for complete test pipeline

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
# Clonning the repo
git clone -b gpus-sycl-siam https://gitlab.lrz.de/hpcsoftware/Peano


# Autotools command
cd Peano libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .

automake --add-missing


# To use with Intel LLVM
./configure CC=clang CXX=clang++ CXXFLAGS="-O3 -fsycl -fsycl-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda -Xsycl-target-backend=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda --cuda-gpu-arch=sm_80" LIBS="-ltbb" LDFLAGS="-fsycl -fsycl-targets=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda -Xsycl-target-backend=nvptx64-nvidia-cuda --cuda-gpu-arch=sm_80"  --with-multithreading=tbb --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing --enable-blockstructured --with-gpu=sycl 


# To use with Intel proprietary compiler
./configure CC=icx CXX=icpx LIBS=-ltbb LDFLAGS="-fsycl -g -tbb" CXXFLAGS="-tbb -O3 -fsycl" -with-multithreading=tbb -enable-exahype -enable-blockstructured -enable-loadbalancing -with-gpu=sycl 


# Build the benchmark (with d=3 and p=8)
cd benchmarks/exahype2/euler/kernel-benchmarks

export PYTHONPATH=../../../../python

python3 kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov.py --dim 3 --patch-size 8


#Run and plot the timings for AoS batched kernel kernel (select your device with --gpus, see available devices by entering sycl-ls to the terminal)
for i in 8; do
	./kernel-benchmarks-fv-rusanov-3d-patch-size-$i --threads 1 --gpus 2 > sycl-3d-ps$i.txt
done

python3 create-exahype-sycl-plot.py -f sycl-3d-ps8.txt -kv batched -t 50 -ds AoS -ps 8 
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~




*/
